<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Apia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Apia is the capital and largest town of Samoa, on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upolu">Upolu</a>. It grew in the 19th century as the main harbour and trading post of the islands, where German, British and American interests competed, and in March 1889 a hurricane wrecked the German and American warships gathered there. It was the seat of German Samoa from 1900 and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> administration of Western Samoa from 1914.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the Tripartite Convention of 1899 partitioned the Kingdom of Samoa, Germany received the western islands of Upolu and Savaiʻi, and the colony was proclaimed at <b>Apia</b> on 1 March 1900.</blockquote><a href="/state/german-samoa">German Samoa</a></li><li><blockquote>As the German firm Godeffroy developed plantations near <b>Apia</b>, Samoan chiefs formed a central government at Mulinuʻu in 1873, with a council of chiefs and an assembly.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-samoa">Kingdom of Samoa</a></li><li><blockquote>Afterwards the League of Nations granted New Zealand a mandate over the territory, governed from 1920 by a civil administrator at <b>Apia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/territory-of-western-samoa">Territory of Western Samoa</a></li></ul></section>
